; A `pure` claim over a row the analysis OVER-approximated.
;
; `handoff` performs nothing. It hands `shout` back, and `shout` is the
; caller's to call or not. Until 2026-08-25 the reference-site rule
; unioned a referent's effects unconditionally, so this drew
; `AX3010 pure claim contradicted: body performs IO` - an accusation
; about a body that does no IO at all. `docs/agent-harness.md` §6 named
; exactly this shape as the reason `AX3010` could not be promoted to an
; error: "promoting that shape refuses correct programs".
;
; WHY THE UNION IS STILL THERE. It is exact for a NULLARY referent -
; this language invokes `vecNew`, `sysArgc` and `__argc` by writing
; their names, so naming one is calling it - and `AX3011` needs the
; upper bound it gives for the rest: `340-effect-op-value.ax` reaches
; `Ask` only through a bare `ask`, and removing the union turned that
; hard error into an `AX3038` warning. Measured, both directions.
;
; So the walk marks the contribution instead. `?:byref` sits beside
; `?:incomplete` and means its opposite - a lower bound versus a
; possible upper one - and only the arm that accuses the AUTHOR reads
; it. `handoff` draws `AX3037`, which is the diagnostic that already
; means "cannot be checked", and that is the truth: whether this
; program performs IO depends on what a caller does with what it got.
;
; `liar` is the control and must still be an `AX3010`. It calls
; `writeStr` itself, no reference involved, and a check that went quiet
; on it would have traded one wrong answer for another.
(import IO)

(:: shout (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (shout n)
  {
    (writeStr 1 "!\n")
    n
  }
)

;@axiom:pure
(:: handoff (-> Int Int Int))

;@axiom:pure
(fn (handoff k) shout)

;@axiom:pure
(:: liar (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:pure
(fn (liar n)
  {
    (writeStr 1 "!\n")
    n
  }
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
